Keywords:                                         Meat and meat products are main sources of human nutrients, including protein, minerals,
Meat                                              vitamins, and fats. One of the main potential risks of meat consumption, to public health, is
Heavy metals                                      the accumulation of heavy metals. Their concentrations in the environment are increasing
Cadmium                                           with the rapid development of human civilization as well as the exploitation of geological
Lead                                              resources. Because they are so prevalent in the environment, heavy metals can infiltrate
Risk assessments                                  the food chain. Food contamination consequently has the potential to negatively impact
                                                  consumer health. Heavy metals, including lead (Pb), cadmium (Cd), and mercury (Hg),
                                                  that are frequently present in food have toxicological reference values, and their primary
                                                  dietary sources are known. Their levels in all kinds of food, including meat, are assessed
                                                  by comparing them with the maximum permissible limits set by the European Union.
                                                  European Commission Regulation EC 2023/915 sets maximum levels (MLs) of heavy
                                                  metals allowed in traded meats from domesticated bovine animals, sheep, pigs, and poul‑
                                                  try, but also from less frequently eaten meats from wild animals, including cephalopods
                                                  and bivalve mollusks.



